1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:05,395
Editing a Sensitive Report Our editor received a draft describing an assault

2
00:00:05,579 --> 00:00:10,566
a hostage incident and a suspected terrorist threat The writer had used

3
00:00:10,579 --> 00:00:15,329
the same dramatic language for all three The editor asked whether each

4
00:00:15,342 --> 00:00:19,513
label was supported and whether the reader could distinguish confirmed information from

5
00:00:19,526 --> 00:00:25,368
an allegation She would not let an adjective substitute for evidence A

6
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:29,829
headline might trigger panic and a careless phrase could propagate an unverified

7
00:00:29,842 --> 00:00:35,697
claim The newsroom had to relay accurate information correct a misleading passage

8
00:00:35,947 --> 00:00:40,579
and avoid turning a person's suffering into a sensation We revised the

9
00:00:40,592 --> 00:00:45,618
text to denote the known facts precisely A direct quotation remained a

10
00:00:45,632 --> 00:00:51,421
quotation it did not become the publication's own conclusion The exercise helped

11
00:00:51,434 --> 00:00:56,947
us understand that word choice can either enlighten or bewilder Responsible reporting

12
00:00:56,961 --> 00:01:00,921
should inform the public without exploiting fear and it should remain open

13
00:01:00,934 --> 00:01:04,039
to correction when subsequent evidence changes the account
